e things.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1128197-strange-places#post1128197\" target=\"_blank\">Strange Places</a> - A collection of Dark, Mysterious, and Wondrous Locations<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/993181-twilight-menagerie\" target=\"_blank\">Twilight Menagerie</a> - A collection of Ephemeral Entities","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/775-reighnhell","name":"Reighnhell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar775_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"749"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-06T11:22:33-08:00","id":1359320,"title":"","content":"Just got done watching the recent Hellboy film.<br /> <br /> While the film isn't very good, the first 15 minutes was an AWESOME inspiration for the World of Darkness' Extreme Wrestling Warfare organization.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-13T15:10:58-08:00","id":1360770,"title":"","content":"<b>Economics and Capitalism </b><br /> <br /> It's a new year, and I thought I might start by going back to the very basics. Economics is one of those things that tend to be a bit mind numbing, and most people really have no idea about how economies work. Thankfully, there are books for that. And even better, books for people who aren't economists (or at least pretend to be ones on TV) and who have little patience for academic jargon. (This is why I'm not going to get into the various &quot;schools&quot; such as Keynesian, Chicago, Austrian, etc., or the differences in micro and macroeconomics.)<br /> <br /> Alfred Mill's <b>Economics 101</b> is part of the Adams 101 series of introductions to various topics, and a decent enough intro to the subject. Also <b>The Economics Book</b>, part of the &quot;Big Ideas Simply Explained&quot; series.<br /> <br /> Going a little deeper, I personally find Thomas Sowell's work well worth reading. He's part of the more libertarian &quot;Chicago School&quot;, but more than that, he's been a prolific writer on economics and the social impact of economic policies for the past several decades. ( I consider his books <i>The Vision of the Anointed</i> and <i>A Conflict of Visions</i> to be must reading for Mage.) His <b>Basic Economics</b> is a fairly easy to read introduction to the topic. Also worth reading is his <b>Economic Facts and Fallacies</b>, and if you really want to dive into the topic, <i>Applied Economics</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Economics in One Lesson</b> is by Henry Hazlitt, part of the &quot;Austrian School&quot;, which also skews libertarian (likely as a response to the various statist forces that had wrecked Europe at the time this was written in 1947, and of the new wave of statists who had occupied Eastern Europe at the time). This is the same &quot;school&quot; of thought that produced Fredrick Hayek and his book <i>Road to Serfdom</i>, also written around this same time.<br /> <br /> While not exactly economics, Steven Levitt and Stephen Dubner's <b>Freakanomics</b> is a really interesting and entertaining look at a number of topics through an economics lens. Including things like why professional sumo wrestling is occasionally fixed and how crack gangs work a lot like fast food franchises. There's also a sequel, <b>Super Freakanomics</b>, as well as two other follow ups, <b>Think Like a Freak</b>, and <b>When to Rob a Bank</b>.<br /> <br /> Regarding capitalism, Adam Smith's <i>Wealth of Nations</i>, written during the Scottish Enlightenment, is one of those cornerstone books of the modern world. Unfortunately, it's a little hard to read. (PJ O'Rourke actually did an entire book about his trying to read Adam Smith, which is easier to read.) Less known is Smith's <i>Theory of Moral Sentiments</i>, about the concepts of morality, virtue, and duty, all of which ended up being a foundation for a lot of what he wrote in Wealth of Nations. (Russ Roberts's <i>How Adam Smith Can Change Your Life</i> describes Moral Sentiments as &quot;the greatest self help book that almost no one had read.&quot;) There are a few children's books about Smith and his economic theories that explain it in a simple and easy fashion. (Weirdly, he's not part of the Who Was biography series, which annoys me as those are usually pretty good.)<br /> <br /> However, most of what we see around the world is not capitalism, but crony capitalism (also known as cronyism, corporatism, or just kleptocratism). This is when people in government power rig the system in favor of their friends or donors. Wealthy individuals or large corporations give politicians large sums of money (but its ok because its a campaign donation so it's not a bribe), and in return, when laws or regulations are drafted, the donees make sure that there are loop holes or exceptions for their friends. Or they make sure their donors get those big government contracts. Sometimes it's more subtle than this. A big company, lets say Wal-Mart, might invent a well paying position on it's board of directors and have the Governor's wife of the state they are headquartered in fill that position. And suddenly Wal-Mart is getting a lot of special favors from the state government. Or say you have a Congressmen who is chairman of a powerful committee who's wife runs a consulting firm, and companies that have business impacted by that committee just happen to hire that consulting firm. Or you have an entire Congress drafting a major bill that will radically overhaul how health insurance is handled in the entire country, and all of them just happen to starting buying and selling their stocks in various health and medical investments just before the bill is voted on. That's crony capitalism, which has about as much to do with real capitalism as The Village People did with punk rock.<br /> While he is probably most (in)famous for his book <i>Clinton Cash</i> (about how that family had turned crony capitalism into an art form), Peter Schweizer has also written other books about political corruption on both sides of the political spectrum, including <b>Throw Them All Out</b>, <b>Extortion</b>, and <b>Secret Empires</b>.<br /> I'm rather irritated that there are no books about the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keating_Five\" target=\"_blank\">Keating Five scandal</a> in the late 80s. For those two young to remember, this was when five US Senators intervened in a federal regulatory investigation of Charles Keating and his Lincoln Savings and Loan after Keating had made over a million dollars (in 1980s money) in campaign donations to the five men. The S&amp;L later collapsed in 89, taking with it over three Billion dollars of government - meaning US tax payers' - money.<br /> <br /> That's all for now. I hope to have something about corporate culture in general done in the next week or so.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 01-13-2020, 08:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-15T12:48:33-08:00","id":1361144,"title":"","content":"And for those who think the modern cigarette industry is bad, I give you <a href=\"http://ace.mu.nu/archives/cocarettes.php\" target=\"_blank\">Cocarettes</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-04T19:31:16-08:00","id":1365433,"title":"","content":"<b>White Collar Corporate and Office Culture</b><br /> <br /> In the real world, corporate offices are often a collection of oppressed cube drones, back stabbing ladder climbers, and high level executives jealously guarding their own fiefdoms while lining their pockets. So, you can imagine how much worse this might be in the World of Darkness, and even more so in a bunch of companies owned and operated by outright sociopaths devoted to controlling, corrupting, or destroying humanity and the world.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of various theories and methods to office management. While the long standing stereotype is the traditionalist model with strict rules, compartmentalization, and major decisions made by the highest ranks, a number of other models have come in to vogue in the business world over the past few decades. This ranges from completely horizontal organizations where everyone, even the CEO, is theoretically equal, and team-oriented set ups where group culture and harmony are prioritized (sometimes even over actual ability), to places where everyone is in competition with each other to make improvements or just advance themselves and places of perpetual uncertainty with often massive employee turnover for one reason or another. Countless articles and books have been written about this over the decades for those who are interested in effective management and leadership. But, in the terms of PENTEX, we are more interested in bad behavior and parody.<br /> <br /> So, one of the most famous examinations/parodies of office cultures in the past few decades is Scott Adams's <b><a href=\"https://dilbert.com\" target=\"_blank\">Dilbert</a></b>. In addition to the long running daily comic strip and an animated series, it has also spawned a number of non-fiction books about office culture. The first one was <b>The Dilbert Principle</b>, named after Adams's concept that companies systematically promote incompetent employees to management in order to get them out of the workflow and keep them from disrupting real business. A similar concept is &quot;The Peter principle&quot;, the idea that people will be promoted until they reach a level at which they are no longer competent. Also very suitable for PENTEX is <b>Dogbert's Top Secret Management Handbook</b>, a tongue in cheek guide to being an evil boss. And then there's <b>The Joy of Work: Finding Happiness at the Expense of Your Co-Workers </b>and <b>Dilbert &amp; The Way of the Weasel</b>.<br /> <br /> The view from the trenches looks very different from that on high. Your average PENTEX office employees are kept in little cubes and made to do mind-numbing paperwork with low pay and not very much in the way of benefits. If you've never seen the film <b>Office Space</b> (1999), you should. It's an entertaining and sarcastic look at a bunch of disgruntled programers facing being downsized and deciding to embezzle money from the company after being laid off. (The ploy is similar to one used by Richard Pryor in <i>Superman III</i>, which isn't a good film, but does have Robert Vaughn as an evil CEO and a malevolent super computer. It's also from the &quot;computers and satellites are magic&quot; era, which is always amusing.) Another personal favorite of mine is <b>9 To 5</b> (1980), about three different office women dealing with their sexist - and it turns out - crooked boss.<br /> <br /> For those looking to climb the corporate ladder, a certain level of ruthlessness is necessary to go along with one's ambition. This is even more true at PENTEX. This is a fairly popular topic in film. <b>Wall Street</b> (1987), with a young up and comer willing to go to any lengths, even illegal ones, to ingratiate himself to a big name financier, is a classic of its decade. While not as ruthless, <b>Working Girl</b> (1988) is a somewhat unfairly forgotten film from that period, with a young working class woman from Staten Island trying to make a place for herself in Manhattan's business world. Also worth watching is <b>Up in the Air</b> (2009), based on the novel by Walter Kim, about a professional &quot;downsizer&quot; who flies around the nation firing employees for other companies. Another good one is <b>Glengarry Glen Ross</b> (1992), involving four real estate salesmen who become increasingly desperate as they face downsizing. And on the more comedic front, <b>The Secret of My Success</b> (1987) is a fun film with Michael J. Fox as a mail room employee masquerading as a midlevel executive. Regarding bad behavior, <b>The Company of Men</b> (1997) is a look at sexism in the corporate world and how amoralism tends to be rewarded. And then there's the poster child for sociopathic corporate behavior, <b>American Psycho</b> (2000), and the Bret Easten Ellis book its based on.<br /> <br /> And at top of the pile are CEOs and boards. These are popular subjects of films. <b>The Hudsucker Proxy</b> (1994) is a film worth watching, involving an underhanded board member trying to take over his own company using a male room clerk turned executive. But the most entertaining is <b>Robocop</b> (1987), with its rather cyberpunk-ish take on corporate politics complete with murder. Also worth watching is the sequel, <b>Robocop 2</b> (1990), which is not as good, but has wonderful examples of backstabbing and throwing people under the bus.<br /> <br /> While most of this deals with American business, I will note three bits about Japanese corporate culture worth looking at. One is <b>Gung Ho</b> (1986), involving a Japanese company trying to reopen a Pennsylvania auto plant, and the culture clashes that result. It's a comedy, and some of the jokes haven't aged well, but it is an interesting look at a period when Japanese industry seemed to dominate and appeared poised to take over America. More serious is <b>Rising Sun</b> (1993), based on the novel by Michael Crichton, involving the conflicts of culture during a murder investigation. Finally, <b>The Bad Sleep Well</b> (1960) is a film from Akira Kirosawa, basically a retelling of Hamlet in which a young man joins a corrupt company in order to gain revenge for the death of his father.<br /> <br /> As far as the supernatural corruption corporate life can cause, <b>Vampire's Kiss</b> (1989) is a look at a yuppie literary agent as he slowly loses his mind and thinks he's turning into a vampire. In the World of Darkness, obviously such a person could really be a vampire, or a ghoul. They could also be a breed of famori that is a literal blood sucking leach. Another worth looking at is the very surreal <b>How To Get Ahead in Business</b> (1989), in which a businessman develops a cyst on his neck that soon starts talking to him and encouraging him to do evil.<br /> <br /> Finally, an anthology of note, <b>Corporate Cthulhu</b>, which is a little hard to find, but is an entertaining collection of Mythos and Mythos-influenced stories set in the corporate world.<br /> <br /> Added: A few additions.<br /> <br /> <b>The Office</b>, both its original British version and the American remake, deals with the day to day activities of employees of a paper company in a mockumentary style. <br /> <br /> While the animated tv series <b>Archer</b> is mainly concerned with espionage and the like (at least in its early seasons), it also involves a lot of dysfunctional workplace drama that would work well at PENTEX companies.<br /> <br /> And I've previously mentioned the film <b>Grandma's Boy</b> (2006), which is kind of a dumb comedy, but for our purposes, the workplace conflict is interesting, and both Joel Moore as a borderline unstable jerk and Kevin Nealon as a boss obsessed with New Age Asian mysticism can both easily serve as inspiration for PENTEX employees.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 02-09-2020, 04:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-18T18:13:31-08:00","id":1368828,"title":"","content":"So, I spent two hours writing a post about professional basketball. And then by hitting the wrong key at the very end, lost the entire thing. So, no update. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Currently eating ice cream.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 02-18-2020, 11:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-20T15:38:30-08:00","id":1369219,"title":"","content":"<b>Professional Basketball (The Readers Digest Version)</b><br /> <br /> So, basketball was invented in 1891 by James Naismith, a Canadian transplant to Massachusetts. If you would like to know more about him and how he invented the game, see Rob Rains's <b>James Naismith: The Man Who Invented Basketball</b>, as well as Naismith's own <b>Basketball: It's Origin and Development</b>.<br /> <br /> It took a while for pro basketball as we know it to develop. By the 1920s, there were a lot of professional teams all over the East Coast and Midwest and elsewhere, but no real organized league. One of the main standouts of this era were the Harlem Globetrotters, who traveled the Midwest playing local teams. By the 1950s, before the modern League, they were one of the biggest basketball teams in the nation (especially after beating the all-white Minneapolis Lakers in 1948). After the NBA came along, they developed into the entertainment spectacle they are famously known for as a way to stay in business. Ben Greene's <b>Spinning the Globe: The Rise, Fall, and Return to Greatness of the Harlem Globetrotters</b> is a good history of the team. There's also the 2005 documentary <b>Harlem Globetrotters: The Team that Changed the World</b>.<br /> <br /> The National Basketball Association is actually from the merger of two briefly existing leagues, the Basketball Association of America and the National Basketball League, in 1949. By the late 50s, they'd firmly established themselves, and were already integrated, with both Asian-American and African-American players. (One of these was Bill Russell, who helped lead the Boston Celtics to a decade long dominance of the league in the 60s; Another was one of his biggest rivals, Wilt Chamberlain, a former Harlem Globetrotter.) A number of teams relocated during this period, including the Lakers moving to LA (where there are no lakes). David Surdam's <b>The Rise of the National Basketball Association</b> is a somewhat scholarly look at this early period, including the League's economic history. Also interesting is John Tyler's <b>The Rivalry: Bill Russell, Wilt Chamberlain, and the Golden Age of Basketball</b>.<br /> <br /> In 1967, the NBA got a rival, the American Basketball Association. The ABA's roster of teams was a little fluid, with a number of teams folding (sometimes getting replaced in the same market the next year, sometimes not) or moving around. (The New Orleans Buccaneers managed to play in three different cities under six different names before folding as the Baltimore Claws in 1975.) In 1976, the ABA was finally able to get the NBA to agree to a merger (becoming the template dream for every upstart sports league since). However, only four of the ABA teams actually got to join the NBA. The rest had either already folded, save two. The owners of the Kentucky Colonels took a buyout, but the owners of the Spirits of St. Louis struck what has to be the single greatest deal in the history of sports, would get a cut of the four ABA-turned-NBA teams' TV revenue in perpetuity. This netted them $300 Million over four decades, before the NBA finally convinced them to take a final $500 Million lump sum to end the deal. Terry Pluto's <b>Loose Balls: The Short Wild Life of the ABA</b> is a good history of the league. Also see <b><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wcaQHlzjDAA\" target=\"_blank\">The Company Man YouTube video</a></b> discussing the differences between the ABA and NBA, and how the ABA strongly influenced the NBA going forward into the 1980s. And, for amusement, there's the 2008 comedy <b>Semi-Pro</b>, with the fictional Flint (Michigan) Tropics trying to be one of the surviving four teams.<br /> <br /> One bit I will mention from the 70s and 80s is LA Laker <i>Kareem Abdul-Jabbar</i>. At over seven feet tall, Kareem was a stand out even in an era of really great players, not least of which for his ability to play the professional game competitively for two decades. One of the things he had attributed this to is that he studied Jeet Kune Do under Bruce Lee and took up yoga in the mid 70s. He's been an actor (appearing in Bruce Lee's 1972 film Game of Death and in the classic comedy Airplane!) and a prolific writer, especially on the topics of civil rights and African-American history (he earned a BA in history at UCLA). I have used him as the inspiration for an Akashic Brotherhood Master in some of my Mage games.<br /> <br /> As far as the modern NBA goes, it's a huge money machine. And not just for teams and their owners, but the players as well. Part of this is due to Oscar Robertson. In addition to being one of the better players of his generation, his lawsuit against the NBA in his role as president of the NBA Players' Association. <i>Robertson v National Basketball Ass'n</i> was an antitrust suit filed in 1970 to stop the ABA and NBA from merging. It was settled in 1976, with part of the settlement being the change to free agency and draft rules, and ultimately much higher salaries for NBA players. Sam Smith's <b>Hard Labor: The Battle that Birthed the Billion Dollar NBA</b> covers the story fairly well.<br /> Elsewhere, Brian Windhorst's <b>LeBron, Inc.: The Making of a Billion Dollar Athlete</b> looks at the evolution of LeBron James from top player to business mogul and international brand name. <b>Elevated: The Global Rise of the NBA</b> is a collection of work by various New York Times columnists over the past four decades covering the League. Pete Croatto's <b>From Hang Time to Prime Time</b> is a history of the NBA during the 70s and 80s that looks interesting, but sadly won't be out until September 2020. Likewise Joel Gunderson's <b>(Inter)National Basketball Association</b>, looking at how the NBA has become an international powerhouse and how the growth of basketball around the world has impacted the NBA (with now about a third of their players coming from outside the United States), which won't be out until October.<br /> <br /> As far as specific teams go, I'll mention the Golden State Warriors. Eric Malinowski's <b>Betaball</b> and Ethan Straus's <b>The Victory Machine</b> both look at the team's recent decade of success and more recent troubles. On the other side of the coin, Mick Minas's <b>The Curse</b> looks at the somewhat troubled history of the LA Clippers.<br /> <br /> There aren't really a lot of films about professional basketball, let alone any with supernatural, sci fi, or criminal overtones. (1996's Space Jam is about it, really.) College ball seems much more popular with film makers. I will mention <i>Teen Wolf</i> (1985), with a werewolf playing high school basketball, and <i>The 6th Man</i> (1997), a rather forgettable film with a dead college player haunting his brother and team.<br /> <br /> (At some point I will talk about women's professional basketball.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 02-20-2020, 07:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-05T07:27:52-08:00","id":1434597,"title":"","content":"I miss the regular updates to this thread. : (<br /> <br /> But I LOVE that it was created in the first place. <br /> <br /> Thanks again, NOoC.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-05T18:17:00-08:00","id":1434657,"title":"","content":"I was planning a post about Social Media, but current events resulted in having to do a lot of additional research. <br /> <br /> I may try to do a post about the Chinese Communist Party and its ties to most modern Western businesses, but it's very likely to turn into an angry diatribe about human rights abuses and political corruption.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-15T07:28:41-08:00","id":1435555,"title":"","content":"Meanwhile, please enjoy <a href=\"http://ace.mu.nu/archives/happy-hour-1765%20(1).jpg\" target=\"_blank\">this classic piece of PENTEX advertising</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T11:07:16-08:00","id":1436046,"title":"","content":"Rather than make a new thread for this, I figure I'd ask it here: how would you go about using Pentex(or any of its subsidiaries) during a game set in the Middle Ages?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T11:30:36-08:00","id":1436048,"title":"","content":"Welcome back \ud83d\ude0a<br /> <br /> I would do it the same way they did with the Cheiron Group in CoD, by making a corporate predecessor to Pentex (and especially Magadon) set in the Middle Ages. There\u2019s a real German Big Pharma company called Fresenius that actually dates back to the mid 1400s, so there is historical precedent for this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T12:25:07-08:00","id":1436058,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436046#post1436046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather than make a new thread for this, I figure I'd ask it here: how would you go about using Pentex(or any of its subsidiaries) during a game set in the Middle Ages?</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not the author of this amazing thread, but I would say, look into craft guilds, production centers in the Middle Ages (such as blademaking in Solingen and especially Flanders), as well as the Italian Merchant Republics. And mining, such as tin mining in England, gold mining in Romania, etc.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T14:23:57-08:00","id":1436072,"title":"","content":"Business corporations did not really exist in the Middle Ages. As legal entities distinct from the people who owned them with limited liability (unlike sole proprietorships or partnerships), they really did not come about until the 1600s. And those were special charters created by crown or parliament to get people to put up money for high risk/high reward enterprises. Universal process of corporations did not come about until much later. There were some exceptions, but not very common. I am not familiar with the Frensenius company, but Wikipedia tells me it was not founded until 1912. It's possible the founder was from a long line of local pharmacists who could date back their store to the 1400s. But that would not be the same thing as it existing as a corporation back then. It's just a family run business.<br /> <br /> Rather than try to shoehorn PENTEX back in the Middle Ages, I think you need to come up with things appropriate for that era. What are you trying to do?<br /> <br /> There are some large scale commercial enterprises back then. But they'd be based on partnerships, not corporations. And mostly limited to trading. There's also family banks in some Italian cities. If all you need is some sort of sinister cabal with lots of money, you can go there. They bring in some strange idol from the east that dates back to Rome or earlier. Occult hi-jincks ensues. Or you could look at some of the larger city merchant or artisan guilds.<br /> <br /> If you want something more like a group that unleashes some kind of evil from the earth, I'd just pick some local mine where the miners digged somewhere they shouldn't. If you need more like that, some Bane controls this group of miners, and sends them outward separately to mine new places to free more banes.<br /> <br /> If you want something where people are dealing with otherworldly entities to make pacts and create fomori, I wouldn't use a business at all. But make it some kind of witches or warlocks coven, or group of learned scholars utilizing some lost manuscript from the Classical era. Scholars could be professors at a medieval university, early alchemists, secluded monks, or an individual collector. Magic potions and poisons can be used as the basis for creating fomori instead of industrial pollution. In legend, the nymph Scylla was turned into a monster by someone poisoning a spring where she bathed.<br /> <br /> If you are looking for some large group that could be involved in many things like this under one roof, I'd just use the Circle of Red as mentioned in Dark Ages: Werewolf.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T19:33:10-08:00","id":1436130,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the ideas, everyone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T03:54:44-08:00","id":1436174,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1177721#post1177721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>SIREN COSMETICS</b><br /> One of the more obscure companies, as the only time they've really been mentioned is in the Enticer fomori breed write up in the various editions of Book of the Wyrm. (For purposes of this thread, I'm treating them like they're there own company; it's entirely possible they could just be a brand name/subsidiary of Young &amp; Smith the way CoverGirl was with Procter &amp; Gamble.)</div> </div> </div> </div>A bit late in the day, however Siren do get some write up in <i>Project Twilight</i> (p72-100).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T08:49:16-08:00","id":1436211,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436072#post1436072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Business corporations did not really exist in the Middle Ages. As legal entities distinct from the people who owned them with limited liability (unlike sole proprietorships or partnerships), they really did not come about until the 1600s. And those were special charters created by crown or parliament to get people to put up money for high risk/high reward enterprises. Universal process of corporations did not come about until much later. There were some exceptions, but not very common. I am not familiar with the Frensenius company, but Wikipedia tells me it was not founded until 1912. It's possible the founder was from a long line of local pharmacists who could date back their store to the 1400s. But that would not be the same thing as it existing as a corporation back then. It's just a family run business.<br /> <br /> Rather than try to shoehorn PENTEX back in the Middle Ages, I think you need to come up with things appropriate for that era. What are you trying to do?<br /> <br /> There are some large scale commercial enterprises back then. But they'd be based on partnerships, not corporations. And mostly limited to trading. There's also family banks in some Italian cities. If all you need is some sort of sinister cabal with lots of money, you can go there. They bring in some strange idol from the east that dates back to Rome or earlier. Occult hi-jincks ensues. Or you could look at some of the larger city merchant or artisan guilds.<br /> <br /> If you want something more like a group that unleashes some kind of evil from the earth, I'd just pick some local mine where the miners digged somewhere they shouldn't. If you need more like that, some Bane controls this group of miners, and sends them outward separately to mine new places to free more banes.<br /> <br /> If you want something where people are dealing with otherworldly entities to make pacts and create fomori, I wouldn't use a business at all. But make it some kind of witches or warlocks coven, or group of learned scholars utilizing some lost manuscript from the Classical era. Scholars could be professors at a medieval university, early alchemists, secluded monks, or an individual collector. Magic potions and poisons can be used as the basis for creating fomori instead of industrial pollution. In legend, the nymph Scylla was turned into a monster by someone poisoning a spring where she bathed.<br /> <br /> If you are looking for some large group that could be involved in many things like this under one roof, I'd just use the Circle of Red as mentioned in Dark Ages: Werewolf.</div> </div> </div> </div>They made a big deal in their marketing materials about being founded in 1444, but they probably weren\u2019t technically a corporation back then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T08:55:56-08:00","id":1436215,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436211#post1436211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They made a big deal in their marketing materials about being founded in 1444, but they probably weren\u2019t technically a corporation back then.</div> </div> </div> </div>They could've been a merchant house, which would be the closest thing in the business world at that time. Maybe part of a larger association, such as the Hanseatic League or the League of Rhenish Towns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T09:04:51-08:00","id":1436220,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jackob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436215#post1436215\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They could've been a merchant house, which would be the closest thing in the business world at that time. Maybe part of a larger association, such as the Hanseatic League or the League of Rhenish Towns.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe a merchant house","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T12:08:58-08:00","id":1436251,"title":"","content":"The <a href=\"https://www.fresenius.com/history\" target=\"_blank\">company website</a> resolves the mystery. A pharmacy called Hirsch Apotheke was founded in Frankfurt in 1462. The Fresenius family took it over in the 19th century. The family proprietor established a pharmaceutical company in 1912 and that's when the corporation began.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T15:28:49-08:00","id":1436301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436251#post1436251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fresenius.com/history&amp;quot;]company</a> website[/URL] resolves the mystery. A pharmacy called Hirsch Apotheke was founded in Frankfurt in 1462. The Fresenius family took it over in the 19th century. The family proprietor established a pharmaceutical company in 1912 and that's when the corporation began.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T16:21:47-08:00","id":1436312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436046#post1436046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather than make a new thread for this, I figure I'd ask it here: how would you go about using Pentex(or any of its subsidiaries) during a game set in the Middle Ages?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, unfortunately(?), most of those industries don't really exist before the Industrial Age, if not the 20th century. Even the really old trades like woodcutting, mining, and farming/livestock probably wouldn't be on a scale that would support a big multi regional conspiracy like PENTEX. I think the more likely avenue for this sort for this sort of thing in the Dark Medieval World/Dark Ages is going to be various Wyrm cults (which is another subject I'd like to cover at some point). <br /> <br /> I mentioned in the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1357074-head-canons-home-brew?p=1373456#post1373456\">Head Canons &amp; Home Brew</a> thread that my own PENTEX set up is a little different than canon, and that several of the companies were founded by and run cults devoted to various Wyrm spirits. Smith &amp; Young I tie directly to the Red Circle, mainly because as I've mentioned, the P&amp;G logo myth just amuses me to no end. Other cults might find certain period suitable expressions, such as That Which Devours All Flesh (Logan Meat Packing Company) being the power behind a secretive clan of cannibal bandits similar to the (probably mythical) Sawney Bean clan. Treerazer (Good House International - and yes, I took it from Pathfinder) may be somehow tied to medieval charcoal burners. The Thirsty Ground/The Ground Which Thirst For Blood (Yale Cane &amp; Fletcher Agriculture) may be tied to rural villages or even fief lords conducting human sacrifices to insure bountiful harvests. And the whole Dagon/Deep Ones stuff I tie to Hallahan Seafood can work with any isolated seaside fishing village along the Atlantic or Baltic. <br /> <br /> But the best bet for a Europe-wide equivalent to PENTEX with its fingers in various courts, the Church, merchant guilds and the like, is to look at Dark Ages: Inquisitor's House of Murnau and use it as the basis for an extended and widespread noble family (like what the Hapsburgs turned into) that is pledged to the Wyrm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-21T05:02:42-08:00","id":1436398,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12890_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12890\" data-vbnamecard=\"12890\">No One of Consequence</a> I love the House of Murnau! So you just switched their original allegiance from the Earthbound to the Wyrm?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-21T18:34:02-08:00","id":1436480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436398#post1436398\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love the House of Murnau! So you just switched their original allegiance from the Earthbound to the Wyrm?</div> </div> </div> </div>You can. I'd probably change the name and tweak parts of their back story as well. I'd maybe have them specifically sworn to the Eater of Souls, with them sometimes being referred to as the House of Consumption, being heavily driven by greed, gluttony, envy and lust. If you wanted to, you could probably have their high tide point be the events in the New World that lead to the loss of the Croatan, with their failure leading to a long down turn and where by the 19th century there's only a small number of them left. <br /> <br /> Really, you can do the same with all of the factions in DA: Inquisitor.<br /> The Red Circle can probably stand in for the Red Order.<br /> The Oculi Dei can become a secretive conspiracy of Wyrm-sworn spies and assassins among the peasantry, merchants and other &quot;invisible&quot; hordes of humanity, probably in service to the Defiler Wyrm. The Oculi Serpentis maybe?<br /> The Poor Knights of the Passion would be a secretive martial order devoted to the Beast of War who have an arsenal of Wyrm fetishes, including weapons and the like. Maybe Knights of Calamity or some such name. Maybe augment them with certain fomori powers when needed. <br /> The Sisters of St. Joan would be maybe some sort of group of Wyrm-tainted or corrupted psychics (possibly even a medieval forerunner of the Braineater fomori breed created using a form of alchemy). Sisterhood of the Unscaled Eye, maybe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-21T18:57:25-08:00","id":1436484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436480#post1436480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You can. I'd probably change the name and tweak parts of their back story as well. I'd maybe have them specifically sworn to the Eater of Souls, with them sometimes being referred to as the House of Consumption, being heavily driven by greed, gluttony, envy and lust. If you wanted to, you could probably have their high tide point be the events in the New World that lead to the loss of the Croatan, with their failure leading to a long down turn and where by the 19th century there's only a small number of them left. <br /> <br /> Really, you can do the same with all of the factions in DA: Inquisitor.<br /> The Red Circle can probably stand in for the Red Order.<br /> The Oculi Dei can become a secretive conspiracy of Wyrm-sworn spies and assassins among the peasantry, merchants and other &amp;quot;invisible&amp;quot; hordes of humanity, probably in service to the Defiler Wyrm. The Oculi Serpentis maybe?<br /> The Poor Knights of the Passion would be a secretive martial order devoted to the Beast of War who have an arsenal of Wyrm fetishes, including weapons and the like. Maybe Knights of Calamity or some such name. Maybe augment them with certain fomori powers when needed. <br /> The Sisters of St. Joan would be maybe some sort of group of Wyrm-tainted or corrupted psychics (possibly even a medieval forerunner of the Braineater fomori breed created using a form of alchemy). Sisterhood of the Unscaled Eye, maybe.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting, but I meant the House of Murnau specifically because before switching sides and joining the Inquisition they had been bound by an Infernal Pact to one of the Earthbound.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-21T20:13:18-08:00","id":1436502,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436484#post1436484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Interesting, but I meant the House of Murnau specifically because before switching sides and joining the Inquisition they had been bound by an Infernal Pact to one of the Earthbound.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, you can probably do that as well. (I'm not sure the Garou would see much distinction between the Earthbound and the truly powerful Wyrm spirits, but if you were doing a crossover game it'd probably come up at some point.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-22T03:15:35-08:00","id":1436528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1436502#post1436502\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, you can probably do that as well. (I'm not sure the Garou would see much distinction between the Earthbound and the truly powerful Wyrm spirits, but if you were doing a crossover game it'd probably come up at some point.)</div> </div> </div> </div>That makes sense. I agree with you that the Garou would probably neither know nor care that the Earthbound weren\u2019t technically \u201cof the Wyrm.\u201d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-23T18:50:38-08:00","id":1436741,"title":"","content":"I'm debating doing a tangental post about current Native American issues, especially involving economic development and land usage. Eventually I want to do my social media entry (if social media companies would ever stop continuing to find new and terrible things to do), as well as about the advertising industry.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T19:43:53-07:00","id":1447517,"title":"","content":"I was plotting out ideas for a Pentex based Amusement Company, Never Never Amusements, the idea was that they focused on making those chintzy mall/amusement park hybrids, naturally located next to housing priced just so that a person working a job can just barely afford to make rent, effective turning it into a corporate village.<br /> <br /> the Company would also host their on Chuck E. Cheese type parlors, with Mascots all inspired by Mockery Breeds and with guest options like Braney the Dinosaur.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T20:05:06-07:00","id":1447518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1194993#post1194993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Avalon </b><b>Incorporated</b><br /> <br /> So, toys.<br /> <br /> Avalon is one of the companies covered in Subsidiaries: A Guide to PENTEX and, in all honesty, I've always felt it was the weakest chapter of the book. (It comes across as an attempt to try to force fit a Changeling/Mage crossover into a Werewolf book. The example toys in the first edition of Book of the Wyrm are way creepier.) That aside, the toy industry is at times a rather fascinating and outright weird place. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasbro\" target=\"_blank\">Hasbro</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mattel\" target=\"_blank\">Mattel</a> are two of the more famous American companies. A few books on the subject include G. Wayne Miller's <b>Toy Wars: The Epic Struggle Between G.I. Joe, Barbie and the Companies That Make Them</b>, Jerry Oppenheimer's <b>Toy Monster: The Big Bad World of Mattel</b>, Sydney Stern and Ted Schoenhaus's <b>Toyland: The High-Stakes Game of the Toy Industry</b>, Eric Clark's <b>The Real Toy Story: Inside the Ruthless Battle for America's Youngest Consumers</b>, Roger Sweet's <b>Mastering the Universe: He-Man and the Rise and Fall of a Billion Dollar Idea<sup>1</sup></b>, Robin Gerber's <b>Barbie and Ruth: The Story of the World's Most Famous Doll and the Woman who Created Her</b>, and <b>Orly Lobel's You Don't Own Me: How Mattel v MGA Entertainment Exposed Barbie's Dark Side</b>.<br /> <br /> Modern Marvels has done an episode on Toys (season 10, episode 85). Also, the documentary <b>Plastic Galaxy: The Story of Star Wars Toys</b> (2014), and the Netflix series <b>The Toys That Made Us</b> (2017; only 4 episodes, unfortunately). You can also find a number of toy-themed channels on YouTube made by hardcore fans of various lines and franchises. From a more fictional angle, there's the 1992 Robin Williams film <b>Toys</b>, which is not all that good but may offer a few ideas. Also, <b>Small Soldiers</b> (1998) and the &quot;Lisa vs Malibu Stacy&quot; episode of The Simpsons (which is were the clip I posted earlier in the thread came from).<br /> <br /> When it comes to the idea of toys - especially dolls - coming to life and attacking people, there's a long and often enjoyable history of the subject. The Twilight Zone's &quot;Living Doll&quot; episode (season five, episode six) is a classic. Stephen King did a variation on the theme with his short story &quot;Battleground&quot;. And, of course, the <b>Child's Play</b> series. (The second film is especially interesting for Avalon purposes as the opening shows both the manufacturing process for the Good Guy dolls and the company executives in full CYA mode in the wake of the first film.) One suspects that Avalon has long made dolls that talk to children and encourage them to do bad things.<br /> <br /> Magic kits are a long time favorite of mine (not that I was ever able to get any of the tricks to work right). Harry Blackstone Jr. was the big name when I was a kid. Chris Angel appears to be selling his own kits now. Such things offer the possibility of introducing kids to Wyrm-craft or possible possession of the psychic banes that create Odyssey subjects.<br /> <br /> Boardgames are a big part of the toy industry, and Tristan Donovan's <b>It's All A Game: The History of Board Games from Monopoly to Settlers of Catan</b> is a good overview of them. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parker_Brothers\" target=\"_blank\">Parker Brothers</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Bradley_Company\" target=\"_blank\">Milton-Bradley</a> were two of the major companies until they were bought out by Hasbro. I suspect that Avalon likes to make board games that encourages the worst aspects of society, including ones where you win by backstabbing and betraying others. I'm sure they also like ones that encourage rampant consumerism. I grew up in the 80s, and one I remember was called Go For It, with the objective of trying to accumulate as much extravagant trappings of wealth and status as possible (in the form of sports cars, high end electronics, huge houses, and the like).<br /> <br /> McFarlane Toys, the money printing machine created by former comic artist Todd McFarlane to fund his sports memorabilia collecting obsession, is slightly infamous for it's horror figures. Their stuff is really aimed more at adults, but I suspect that most of Avalon's action figure lines look like the stuff McFarlane makes. (Thanks to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1173903#post1173903\">Fat Larry</a> for reminding me about them.)<br /> <br /> I can remember the Collectable Card Game boom of the mid-90s, including stories of people getting mugged for boxes of Magic: The Gathering Cards. One can easily imagine banes feeding on and encouraging such behavior. Likewise using Cute &amp; Fuzzy Cockfighting Seizure Monsters to encourage kids to trap small animals and force them to fight to the death for their amusement<br /> <br /> I may as well mention <b>Mighty Morphin Power Rangers: The Movie</b> (1995), with Ivan Ooze's mind controlling ooze as a potential angle for the toy slime that seems so popular with kids (it's allure always escaped me, for some reason). Kids play with it and it ends up taking over their parents, Invasion of the Body Snatchers style.<br /> <br /> And, finally, I've previously mentioned my love for Grant Morrison's short bit <b>&quot;New Toys&quot;</b> from the Vertigo miniseries Weird War Tales DC Comics did nearly 20 years ago. <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The reveal at the end is that kids toys are being replaced by insectoid like aliens for malevolent purposes. In my own personal WoD, I like to insinuate that Avalon is secretly run by Ramsey Campbell's Insects from Shaggai.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> ADDED: I'd nearly forgotten about one of my favorite action figure parodies, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/user/getkonnected\" target=\"_blank\">The Kronies</a>, teaching kids the importance of all the crony corporate pseudo-capitalism that big-business/big-government collusion can engineer.<br /> <br /> Also, I added a bit about the 1976 remake of King Kong under the original Endron post.<br /> <br /> And let's all have a moment of silence for the passing of Toys R Us.<br /> <br /> <br /> (Hope to have a bit about the meat packing industry done for the weekend. Then probably video games/computers, cigarettes, and Project: Odyssey in some order.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <sup>1</sup>If I'd known used copies of this were going to be going for over $100 bucks on Amazon, I'd've kept mine when I read it ten years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> An often Overlooked movie that could provide good inspiration is the film <b>Small Soldiers, </b>which features Toys programmed with chips that turn them sapient, not only a good idea for more comical takes on bane possessed toys, but the Primary antagonists, the Commando Elite (billed as the in-universe toyline's heroes) serve as a great visual inspiration for depictions of Action bill characters. Just replace the Gorgonites with Comically exaggerated Garou and Fera stereotypes for the villains and its a perfect propaganda line.<br /> <br /> As Freak Legion pointed out, it doesn't even take much exaggeration to paint the Garou as Villains, a Bunch of Savage monsters bent on Sending humanity back to the stone age or even extinction itself, depending on the Tribe, its not even a slight exaggeration.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-06T22:49:34-07:00","id":1447768,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crazyjabberwock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1447517#post1447517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was plotting out ideas for a Pentex based Amusement Company, Never Never Amusements, the idea was that they focused on making those chintzy mall/amusement park hybrids, naturally located next to housing priced just so that a person working a job can just barely afford to make rent, effective turning it into a corporate village.<br /> <br /> the Company would also host their on Chuck E. Cheese type parlors, with Mascots all inspired by Mockery Breeds and with guest options like Braney the Dinosaur.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't forget to take a page from Five Nights At Freddy's, and have the Mockery Mascots be able to walk around at night and murder intruders. Possibly while being possessed by either 1) Banes or 2) the captured ghosts of the park's murder victims. Because Wyrm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T04:50:25-07:00","id":1447791,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1447768#post1447768\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't forget to take a page from Five Nights At Freddy's, and have the Mockery Mascots be able to walk around at night and murder intruders. Possibly while being possessed by either 1) Banes or 2) the captured ghosts of the park's murder victims. Because Wyrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean the new security system after hours at O'Toolley's Neverland Party Place? I thought it was still in testing.<br /> Because the overlap is enough that I can see it as a joint venture.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T18:59:38-07:00","id":1447879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1447791#post1447791\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You mean the new security system after hours at O'Toolley's Neverland Party Place? I thought it was still in testing.<br /> Because the overlap is enough that I can see it as a joint venture.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had actually been planning to include a Rollypolly O'Tolly Animatronic Inspired in appearance by Balloon Boy, but grown massive by the bane Possessing it, with a leech like maw in its belly, its fighting style would be derived from Stitches the Abomination (WoW.HoTS character) along with some others of course.<br /> <br /> In the segment these Banetronics as I call them, have actually gone rogue and its a 3 way melee between the Players, the Machines and a First team.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T22:32:49-07:00","id":1447893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crazyjabberwock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1447879#post1447879\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I had actually been planning to include a Rollypolly O'Tolly Animatronic Inspired in appearance by Balloon Boy, but grown massive by the bane Possessing it, with a leech like maw in its belly, its fighting style would be derived from Stitches the Abomination (WoW.HoTS character) along with some others of course.<br /> <br /> In the segment these Banetronics as I call them, have actually gone rogue and its a 3 way melee between the Players, the Machines and a First team.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds so cool. I love it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-08T15:50:15-07:00","id":1447994,"title":"","content":"On the topic of animatronic pizza parlor/arcades for kids (apparently the official term is &quot;family entertainment centers&quot; or FEC), The Company Man <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u6L65O4YC08\" target=\"_blank\">has a video about the history and recent bankruptcy of Chuck E. Cheese</a>.<br /> <br /> And while I'm only aware of Five Nights at Freddy's via cultural osmosis, attempts to make a movie adaption kept stalling out for various reasons. But in the mean time there's been the fairly similar <b>Banana Splits Movie</b> (2019) and <b>Willy's Wonderland</b> (2021). <br /> <br /> Dave &amp; Busters makes a good template for the concept aimed at adults. Especially if you incorporate things from King Breweries and the various video gaming companies. <br /> <br /> Chains of bowling alleys, mini golf, and go-kart tracks probably also work as potential PENTEX investments. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-09T21:03:19-07:00","id":1448095,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Willy's Wonderland was actually what pushed me into pondering the idea in the first place, My main debate is how to build them out of Fomori Stats and how powerful to make them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26780-crazyjabberwock","name":"Crazyjabberwock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26780_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-21T10:09:33-07:00","id":1449194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1252903#post1252903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>CONSOLIDEX WORLDWIDE</b><br /> <br /> <i><b>Randolph Duke:</b> Now, some of our clients are speculating that the price of gold will rise in the future; And we have other clients who are speculating that the price of gold will fall. They place their orders with us, and we buy \u2013 or sell \u2013 their gold for them.<br /> <b>Mortimer Duke:</b> Tell him the good part.<br /> <b>Randolph:</b> The good part, William, is that, no matter whether our clients make money or lose money, Duke &amp; Duke get the commissions.<br /> <b>Mortimer:</b> Well, what do you think, Valentine?<br /> <b>Billy Ray Valentine:</b> Sounds to me like you guys a couple of bookies.<br /> <b>Randolph:</b> I told you he\u2019d understand.</i><ul><li><i><b>Trading Places (1983)</b></i></li> </ul><br /> A <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brokerage_firm\" target=\"_blank\">brokerage firm</a> who deal in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_(finance)\" target=\"_blank\">financial securities</a>, which can include corporate stocks, bank notes, debts, commodities, and a number of other things. That\u2019s the bare bones of what they are. The actual ins and outs of financial trading is almost mind-numbing with all of it\u2019s variations, special rules and loopholes. (It resembles both the law and government bureaucracy in this way, and if one were extremely cynical, they might suspect that all three fields have been designed that way in order to make them impenetrable to outsiders and preserve the special status of those on the inside.) Hence, people hire brokers to help invest their money for them. In the case of Consolidex, that money, more often than not, gets invested in companies owned by PENTEX. That\u2019s the way the company primarily serves the Wyrm. (There are a few others, but I\u2019ll get into them a little later.)<br /> <br /> For those wanting to get a better understanding of the basics of financial trading, the <b>ADAMS 101</b> book series has a pair of books on <b>Investing</b> and the <b>Stock Market</b> that are a pretty good introduction. (They\u2019ve also got ones on Economics, Day Trading, Accounting and many other subjects, so you can case that particular trail as far as your heart desires.) There\u2019s also books like Joel Kurtzman\u2019s <b>How the Markets Really Work</b>, Kirk Meyer\u2019s <b>How the Stock Market Operates</b>, and Even Houpt &amp; John Border\u2019s <b>Stock Market for Beginners Book</b>. Related would be DK\u2019s<b> How Money Works</b> and Oxford University Press\u2019s <b>Banking: A Very Short Introduction</b>. (The Very Short Introduction series also has a huge number of books on various economic and other subjects.) And finally, John Kay\u2019s <b>Other People\u2019s Money: The Real Business of Finance</b>, which is a really good overview of the history of the financial sector, how it\u2019s recovered from the 2008 crisis, and a lot of the problems it had and still has.<br /> <br /> The financial market isn\u2019t exactly a new thing (though I sort of suspect that Consolidex, at least in it\u2019s current form, probably dates back to the 80s or 90s). If you\u2019re interested in it\u2019s history, Edward Chancellor\u2019s<b> Devil Take the Hindmost: A History of Financial Speculation</b> is a good read, going back as far as the Roman Empire and looking at how the modern markets developed. I\u2019m personally a big fan of Niall Ferguson\u2019s work, which includes <b>The Ascent of Money</b> and <b>The House of Rothschild</b> (about what is probably the single wealthiest family dynasty in world history). Ron Chernow\u2019s <b>The House of Morgan</b> is a good biography of JP Morgan and his dynasty\u2019s place in the development of American banking and finance. Liaquat Ahamed\u2019s<b> Lords of Finance</b> is about the bankers who helped start the Great Depression.<br /> <br /> The 1980s were when the stock market became \u201ca thing\u201d and the obnoxiously greedy yuppie stock broker became one of the faces of the decade. Bryan Burrough &amp; John Helyar\u2019s <b>Barbarians at the Gate</b> is a classic work of non-fiction about Wall Street in the 1980s. There\u2019s a pretty good <i>1993 movie adaption</i> of it. Also, and especially fitting for a PENTEX brokerage firm, is Brett Easton Ellis\u2019s <b>American Psycho</b>, as well as it\u2019s <i>2000 movie version</i>. <b>Other People\u2019s Money</b> (1991) is about buying up companies just to liquidate their assets. Michael Lewis\u2019s <b>Liar\u2019s Poker</b> is a good non-fiction book. And while I\u2019m not really a fan of Oliver Stone\u2019s work, the movie<b> Wall Street</b> (1987) isn\u2019t bad. One the other hand, <b>Trading Places</b> (1983) has long been one of my favorite films, not least of which for Don Ameche and Ralph Bellamy as the Dukes. They\u2019ve long been one of my role models for wealthy manipulative puppet masters, partially because their motivations are so petty.<br /> As we start to drift into the 1990s, the big one is Jordan Belfort\u2019s autobiography, <b>The Wolf of Wall Street </b>and it\u2019s<i> 2013 movie adaption</i>. There\u2019s also 2000\u2019s <b>The Boiler Room</b>, which I\u2019ve never seen, but it\u2019s subject of crooked brokerage companies and stock schemes is very appropriate. Overseas, there\u2019s Nick Leeson\u2019s autobiography <b>Rogue Trader</b>, about how he more or less single handedly destroyed Barrings Bank. There\u2019s a <i>1999 movie adaption</i> if it as well.<br /> In the 21<sup>st</sup> century, there\u2019s an almost overwhelming number of books about the financial sector and it\u2019s recent history. Just to hit a few of the high points, Bethany McLean &amp; Peter Elkind\u2019s <b>The Smartest Guys in the Room</b> (and it\u2019s <i>2005 film version</i>), Roger Lowenstein\u2019s <b>When Genius Failed</b>, Andrew Sorkin\u2019s <b>Too Big To Fail</b>, Michael Lewis\u2019s <b>The Big Short</b> (and it\u2019s <i>2015 film version</i>) and <b>Flash Boys</b>, Scott Patterson\u2019s <b>The Quants</b>, Scott Wapner\u2019s <b>When Wolves Bite</b>, the movie <b>Margin Call </b>(2011) and the documentary<b> Inside Job</b> (2010).<br /> <br /> Regarding the more criminal aspects of finance and Wall Street, Bastian Obermayer &amp; Frederik Obermaier\u2019s <b>The Panama Papers</b> revealed the underground networks in which the super wealthy hide their money. Sheelah Kolhatkar\u2019s <b>Black Edge</b> looks at insider trading. <b>Dark Pools</b> is about underground trading networks and automated trading bots (which in the WoD could easily be spirit-based \u201cAIs\u201d). A number of the material above also deals with various financial schemes, especially the practice of &quot;pump &amp; dump&quot; (hyping a stock - usually a bad one - just to inflate the price so you can unload it for profit). Odds are that Consolidex involves itself in all of these things.<br /> <br /> Finally, for a bit of cheese, I offer the werewolf film <b>Wolves of Wall Street</b> (2002).<br /> <br /> A lot of this information is equally suitable for aspects of the Glass Walkers (especially the Corporate Wolves camp), and for Mage\u2019s Syndicate (and parts of the Virtual Adepts), as well as Vampire games involving Wall Street or high finance.<br /> Obviously, most of this focuses on the American financial markets, but other countries have their own, including massive ones in Europe and Asia. If anyone from around the globe can offer any tips or information on their nation\u2019s markets, I\u2019d be interested in seeing it.<br /> <br /> Added: I forgot to mention the film<b> Pi</b> (1998), which is a very good film involving attempts to mathematically predict the stock market, Hebrew Gematria/numerology, and Wall Street conspiracies.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I think a Consolidex writeup for the Employee Indoctrination Manual was the next stretchgoal we didn't make for BotW20, and the blurb for the stretchgoal also mentioned pay day lenders, which is a weird thing to cram under the same umbrella, but definitely should be in Pentex's wheel house.<br /> <br /> I'm inclined to play Consolidex more toward street level play than Wallstreet. Payday lending, credit cards, and collections. Probably well linked up with offshore online gambling as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-21T10:12:00-07:00","id":1449195,"title":"","content":"As for how I'd run Magadon: Way less anti-vaxx fantasy, way more reality of Purdue and the Sacklers. Opioids, Opioids, Opioids.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T20:38:19-07:00","id":1449311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1173822-pentex-research-inspiration-company-by-company?p=1449195#post1449195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for how I'd run Magadon: Way less anti-vaxx fantasy, way more reality of Purdue and the Sacklers. Opioids, Opioids, Opioids.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my own head canon, Magadon Medical &amp; Pharmaceutical, which has gobbled up most of the other PENTEX medical-related companies, primarily spreads misery by pushing utilitarian bioethics (including aspects of eugenics), uncaring health-care bureaucracy and abuse (especially through insurance, hospitals, rehab programs, and assisted living facilities), organ and body part harvesting (and sales), clinics with little or no oversight in areas such as sanitation or licensing, counterfeit drugs, defective medical equipment, bribing/co-opting various health and oversight agencies (CDC, FDA, WHO, etc.), animal testing, bribing doctors to use their drugs, encouraging vanity-based plastic surgery, and potentially dangerous research into infectious diseases. Vaccination conspiracies never really come up. <br /> While writer Robin Cook's work often varied wildly in quality, I find a lot of his plot ideas to be really good hooks for potential Magadon shenanigans. (Coma, Mortal Fear, Terminal, Godplayer, and Acceptable Risk are some of the ones that especially stand out in my memory.) There's also a number of Law &amp; Order episodes that have some good ideas. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-24T17:01:04-08:00","id":1468795,"title":"","content":"Not dead, just sleeping. (That or pining for the fjords.) <br /> <br /> An <a href=\"https://arstechnica.com/cars/2021/11/was-1980-the-worst-automotive-year-ever/\" target=\"_blank\">interesting bit about why 1980 was arguably the worst year for the American auto industry</a>, including a brief overview of how it got to that point.<br /> <br /> And Jack Ewing's <b>Faster, Higher, Farther: How One of the World's Largest Automakers Committed a Massive and Stunning Fraud</b>, about the Volkswagen emissions scandal that was uncovered a couple of years ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-21T16:37:50-08:00","id":1471051,"title":"","content":"Something I came across today, <a href=\"https://www.inc.com/minda-zetlin/hertz-530-million-lawsuit-customers-arrested-bankruptcy-court.html\" target=\"_blank\">involving a recent suit against Hertz rental car company</a>, and how it reflects a sort of corporate mindset which may be far too common.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile,<a href=\"http://ace.mu.nu/archives/CigMom.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">